WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.840
YURI: Do you develop distributed
applications, or systems made

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.580
up of lots of microservices?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.010
If so, you've surely
had to deal with issues

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.770
where users complain about
the service being slow.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.510
In a distributed
system it's often

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.490
hard to figure out which
dependency is causing it.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.790
This is where distributed
tracing comes in.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.610
Recover using OpenTelemetry
to capture and analyze latency

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.040
from distributed applications
in the previous episode.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.832
If you haven't watched
it, we'll link to it

00:00:24.832 --> 00:00:25.830
in the episode notes.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.220
But in brief, we want to create
traces for user requests,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.680
and spans for the service,
the service requests

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.890
whose latency we want to analyze
to determine which services may

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.590
be contributing the most
to the overall latency

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.210
experienced by our users.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.310
OpenTelemetry is an
open source library

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.650
that allows us to easily
instrument our services,

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.390
and capture traces from.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.170
My name is Yuri.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.790
I'm an SRE at Google Cloud.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.080
And today, I'm going
to show you two ways

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.385
to instrument your services
with OpenTelemetry.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:53.760
I'll cover easily
getting started

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.860
with automatic
instrumentation, and using

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.290
manual instrumentation
for maximum control.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.360
Welcome back to Engineering
for Reliability.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.250
Let's have a look at a
simple application that

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.410
has a front end that
serves user requests,

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.450
and makes its own
request to a back end

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.170
to retrieve additional data.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.360
We'll use this for both
of our examples today.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.870
So ideally, we want
to capture the latency

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.550
of the overall
request as perceived

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.220
by the user, the latency
of the back end request

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.920
as seen by the front end,
and how long the back end

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.080
processing takes.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.420
The front end of our
application is written in Flask,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.660
and it uses a request library
to make back end calls.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.820
The back end is
also a Flask app.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.100
When it receives a request,
it does some processing

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.960
before returning a response.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.940
To use automatic
instrumentation,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.100
we can run both our
front end and back end

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.590
using the OpenTelemetry
instrument command.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.660
The command will try
to automatically detect

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.660
packages use bar code, and
apply automatic tracing

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.010
instrumentation on them.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.680
We get distributed tracing
exported to Cloud Trace

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.960
without having to
make any code changes.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.030
Let's take a look.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:02.600
Our front end is running
locally using the OpenTelemetry

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.590
instrument command,
instructing you

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.460
to send tracers to Cloud Trace.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.470
And the back end is
running locally as well

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.110
using the same command.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.920
Let's send a request
to our application

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:15.962
and confirm the response.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.770
In the Cloud Trace UI, we
can find the resulting trace.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.750
It's comprised of
multiple spans.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:31.110
The root span is
the overall request.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.920
And we can see how long it took.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.930
It was made to our service
running a local host

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:37.650
on port 8080.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.500
The first child span
is the back end request

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.300
that was also made
to local host,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.040
but on port 8081, which
is where our back end

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.420
service is running.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.700
So this is how you
can use OpenTelemetry

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.830
automatic instrumentation
to capture traces

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.890
across distributed
services without the need

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.920
to modify application
code at all.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.160
That was really easy.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.170
But there is a caveat.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.140
We couldn't create a custom
span to time back end processing

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.490
without adding
instrumentation in the code.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.580
So let's have a look
at how you can manually

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.890
instrument your service
to explicitly control

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.600
how spans are created.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.340
We'll link to the full code
sample in the episode notes.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.630
But let's start by reviewing
our front end code.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.240
First, we need to import
the necessary OpenTelemetry

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.850
libraries that will let us
manage spans and context

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.220
propagation between
our services.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.560
We'll use the request
context to send information

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.930
about our trace to the
back end, so that it

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.130
can append its own spans to it.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:41.230
Next, we configure our exporter
to send traces to Cloud Trace.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.110
Finally, we're ready to
make our back end request.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:45.730
We create our root
span that will

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.400
serve as the parent for
the subsequent spans.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.590
Next, we create a
span specifically

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.320
to time how long the
back end request takes.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.170
Lastly, we make a
request with HTTP headers

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.560
that will carry the spam
context to our back end.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.480
In our back end
service, we first

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.900
extract the spam context from
the incoming request headers.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.800
We then create a dedicated
spam using that context to time

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.350
how long our back end
processing task takes.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.900
Now, let's run our services
and inspect our trace

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.149
in Cloud Trace.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:19.450
In this example, we also have
our front end running locally.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.240
But this time, we're using
the basic Python command.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.510
Our back end is also running
locally using the same command.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.010
We can confirm that
our application

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:32.780
is responding by
sending a request

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.640
and confirming the response.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.820
Now, we can go to Cloud
Trace and find the resulting

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.110
trace in the trace list.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.466
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:56.560
The trace has our root span, the
back end request span, and now,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.160
a dedicated span for
back end processing.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.260
For each of these, we
can see exactly how long

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.690
they took, which
allows us to figure out

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:08.930
exactly where the time is spent
in serving the user request.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.170
So this is how you can
use manual instrumentation

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:15.040
with OpenTelemetry to send a
request context over the wire,

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.000
and use it to a open child
spans from downstream services.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.470
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.810
We learned how to use
both automatic and manual

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.000
instrumentation with
OpenTelemetry for distributed

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.940
tracing of our Python services,
and understand latency

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.842
in distributed application.

00:05:29.842 --> 00:05:32.050
I hope this helps you to
keep your services reliable,

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:33.490
and your users happy.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.830
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.680
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.310
See you soon.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.960
